# Target C: diameter-density-sensitive square-root braid

**Date:** 2026-10-02\
**Base:** current October cubic-incidence + Woodall-cover stack\
**Status:** proof-only consequence. C-II and Level I remain open.

## 1. Input

Let \(H\) be the full actual diameter graph of the canonical post-ZF C-II source.
Write

\[
h=|E(H)|,\qquad \nu=\nu(H),\qquad \tau=\tau(H).
\]

Current main supplies the cubic incidence theorem. If \(S\) is a set of \(N\)
actual diameter vertices receiving \(J\) endpoint incidences from a chosen family
of actual diameter edges, then

\[
(J-N)_+^3<\mathcal B_DN^2,
\tag{1}
\]

where

\[
\frac{\mathcal B_D}{D^2}\longrightarrow 12\pi\sqrt3.
\tag{2}
\]

The Woodall topology gives the near-König cover bound

\[
\boxed{\tau\le\nu+1.}
\tag{3}
\]

The global square-root braid theorem used only the weak density input
\(h/D\ge1-o(1)\). Keeping the actual diameter-edge density produces a stronger
coefficient.

## 2. Density-sensitive matching theorem

### Theorem 2.1

For every unbounded canonical post-ZF C-II sequence,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}\frac{\nu(H)}{\sqrt D}
\ge
\frac{
\left(\displaystyle\liminf_{D\to\infty}\frac hD\right)^{3/2}
}{
\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}
}.
}
\tag{4}
\]

More generally, on any subsequence on which

\[
\frac hD\to y>0,
\]

one has

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{\nu(H)}{\sqrt D}
\ge
\frac{y^{3/2}}{\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}}.
}
\tag{5}
\]

### Proof

Take a minimum vertex cover \(S\) of \(H\) and put

\[
N:=|S|=\tau(H).
\]

Every diameter edge has at least one endpoint in \(S\), so its endpoint incidence
count satisfies

\[
J:=\sum_{v\in S}d_H(v)\ge h.
\tag{6}
\]

Fix a subsequence on which \(\nu/\sqrt D\) tends to its liminf. If that liminf is
infinite there is nothing to prove. Otherwise

\[
\nu=O(\sqrt D).
\]

By (3),

\[
N\le\nu+1=O(\sqrt D),
\qquad
\frac ND\to0.
\tag{7}
\]

Pass further so that \(h/D\to y\). Then \(y\) is at least the global
\(\liminf h/D\). For large \(D\), \(J\ge h>N\), and (1) gives

\[
(h-N)^3
\le
(J-N)^3
<
\mathcal B_DN^2.
\tag{8}
\]

Divide by \(D^3\):

\[
\left(\frac hD-\frac ND\right)^3
<
\frac{\mathcal B_D}{D^2}
\frac{N^2}{D}.
\tag{9}
\]

Using (2), (7), and \(h/D\to y\),

\[
y^3
\le
12\pi\sqrt3\,
\liminf\frac{N^2}{D}.
\]

Thus

\[
\liminf\frac N{\sqrt D}
\ge
\frac{y^{3/2}}{\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}}.
\]

Finally \(\nu\ge N-1\) by (3), so the same asymptotic lower bound holds for
\(\nu/\sqrt D\). QED.

## 3. Near-circle low-detour specialization

Now assume the low-outer-detour endpoint

\[
x_B=o(D)
\]

and remain in the square-root matching regime

\[
\nu=O(\sqrt D).
\tag{10}
\]

The canonical near-circle theorem gives

\[
\frac qD\to\pi.
\tag{11}
\]

Woodall gives

\[
d_H=q-h\le\nu=O(\sqrt D)=o(D),
\tag{12}
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\frac hD\to\pi.}
\tag{13}
\]

Insert \(y=\pi\) into Theorem 2.1:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{\nu(H)}{\sqrt D}
\ge
\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{12\sqrt3}}.
}
\tag{14}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{12\sqrt3}}
=0.689\ldots .
\]

This is much stronger than the density-one global constant

\[
\frac1{\sqrt{12\pi\sqrt3}}
=0.123\ldots .
\]

The improvement is not a new geometric inequality; it is the same cubic incidence
budget applied at the actual near-circle diameter-edge density.

## 4. Exact braid consequence

A maximum matching of size \(\nu\) consists of pairwise vertex-disjoint actual
diameter chords. The diameter-thrackle property makes them pairwise properly crossing,
and the exact cyclic-order lemma makes the matching one offset pairing on its
\(2\nu\) selected hull endpoints.

Therefore the near-circle square-root branch contains an exact two-shore crossing
braid of size

\[
\boxed{
\left(
\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{12\sqrt3}}-o(1)
\right)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{15}
\]

The current midpoint-energy theorem still puts all braid midpoints in one
\(O(D^{2/3})\) disk.

## 5. Interaction with the exact leaf-fan theorem

Current main's leaf-fan / outer-walk fork gives

\[
A+x_B+3\nu\ge h,
\tag{16}
\]

where \(A\) counts exact loaded-hull unit contacts whose two diameter-leaf endpoints
share the same diameter carrier.

Under (10)--(13),

\[
x_B=o(D),\qquad
\nu=O(\sqrt D),\qquad
h=\pi D-o(D),
\]

so (16) sharpens to

\[
\boxed{
A=\pi D-o(D)
}
\tag{17}
\]

because trivially \(A\le q=\pi D+o(D)\).

Thus almost every loaded-hull adjacency is an exact \((D,D,1)\) same-carrier fan
contact.

If \(c\) is the number of nonleaf diameter carriers supporting leaves, then

\[
c\le\nu.
\tag{18}
\]

Consequently the average fan block contains at least

\[
\frac{A}{c}
\ge
\left(
\sqrt{12\sqrt3}-o(1)
\right)\sqrt D
\tag{19}
\]

exact fan contacts whenever the matching is asymptotically at the minimum
coefficient (14). Numerically the coefficient is about \(4.56\).

This does not assert that every carrier has that order or that the lower bound in
(14) is attained. It identifies the balanced hard endpoint forced by simultaneous
near-saturation of the cubic incidence and leaf-fan budgets.

## 6. What remains

The low-detour square-root branch is now simultaneously:

- \(h,q=\pi D-o(D)\);
- \(\nu\ge(0.689\ldots-o(1))\sqrt D\);
- \(D-o(D)\) diameter leaves and \(O(\sqrt D)\) nonleaf carriers;
- \(A=\pi D-o(D)\) exact same-carrier unit hull contacts;
- a crossing braid of the same \(0.689\sqrt D\) scale;
- and, by the weighted-cover theorem on the companion branch, an actual contact
  multiplier of order \(D/\nu=\Omega(\sqrt D)\).

The remaining gap is not cardinality. A closing theorem has to show that an
almost-complete loaded hull assembled from only \(O(\sqrt D)\) exact radius-\(D\)
fan arcs cannot support the required full-core contact/attachment system without a
unit triangle or a source-preserving diameter descent.

**Disposition:** proof-only consequence. No computation, numerical search, verifier,
or CI output is used.

